home=f'''<div class="wrap"><section class="hero"><div class="hero-copy"><span class="eyebrow">Menopause & intimate care</span><h1>Your body changes.<br><em>You deserve clarity.</em></h1><p>Noticing a change in scent? Understand what may be happening, compare gentle external washes, and get straight answers about probiotics.</p><div class="actions">{btn('/external-washes/','Compare gentle washes')}{link('/odor-and-menopause/','Start with the basics','text-link')}</div></div><figure class="hero-visual"><img src="/assets/routine.webp" alt="Soft white towels and an amber dispenser in warm bathroom light" fetchpriority="high"><div class="floating-note"><strong>Care, without<br>the pressure.</strong><span>A natural scent is normal. A special wash is optional.</span></div><figcaption class="photo-note">Illustrative setting, not recommended products. Photo: Yukon Haughton / Unsplash.</figcaption></figure></section><div class="trustline"><span>Sources you can check</span><span>Labels explained plainly</span><span>No odor-cure promises</span></div><section class="section"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">Find your starting point</span><h2>What would help today?</h2></div><p>Useful answers, whether you’re here to learn, compare, or decide what to ask your clinician.</p></div><div class="topic-grid"><a class="topic" href="/odor-and-menopause/"><img src="/assets/towels.webp" alt="Folded towels beside a dispenser" loading="lazy"><div class="topic-body"><span class="eyebrow">Understand</span><h3>“Why has my<br>scent changed?”</h3><p>Menopause, external skin, and symptoms worth checking.</p><span class="text-link">Get some clarity ↗</span></div></a><a class="topic" href="/external-washes/"><img src="/assets/love-cleanser.webp" alt="Love Wellness external cleanser packaging" style="object-fit:contain;background:#E3E9DF;padding:16px" loading="lazy"><div class="topic-body"><span class="eyebrow">Compare</span><h3>“What can I<br>wash with?”</h3><p>Fragrance, intended use, and what a wash can actually do.</p><span class="text-link">Compare the labels ↗</span></div></a><a class="topic" href="/probiotics/"><img src="/assets/uro-gummy.webp" alt="URO gummy bottle, an example discussed in our evidence guide" style="object-fit:contain;background:#F0DED9;padding:16px" loading="lazy"><div class="topic-body"><span class="eyebrow">Look closer</span><h3>“Do probiotics<br>really help?”</h3><p>Separate an ingredient claim from a result that matters.</p><span class="text-link">Read the evidence ↗</span></div></a></div></section></div><section class="section sage-band"><div class="wrap split"><div><span class="eyebrow">Before you add to cart</span><h2>You don’t need to<br>wash away being human.</h2><p>The vulva is outside. The vagina is inside. Routine care is about gentle external cleansing, not internal washing or covering a natural scent.</p><p>A new or persistent change, especially with discharge, burning, itching, or pain, deserves a clinician’s attention.</p><div class="actions">{btn('/gentle-care/','Read the gentle-care guide',True)}{link('/when-to-get-checked/','When to get checked','text-link')}</div></div><div><div class="evidence-row"><span class="number">01</span><div><h3>Start simply</h3><p>Water is sufficient for many people. A tolerated fragrance-free cleanser is optional.</p></div></div><div class="evidence-row"><span class="number">02</span><div><h3>Read the whole label</h3><p>“Sensitive,” “natural,” and “pH balanced” don’t answer every ingredient question.</p></div></div><div class="evidence-row"><span class="number">03</span><div><h3>Keep symptoms separate from shopping</h3><p>A wash cannot diagnose or treat the cause of a new vaginal odor.</p></div></div></div></div></section><div class="wrap"><section class="section"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">The label comparison</span><h2>Gentle-care options,<br>with the details that matter.</h2></div>{link('/external-washes/','See the full comparison ↗','text-link')}</div>{disclosure()}<div class="product-grid">{''.join(product(p) for p in shortlist)}</div></section></div><section class="section rose-band"><div class="wrap split"><div><span class="eyebrow">The probiotic question</span><h2>A gummy and a capsule<br>aren’t the same evidence.</h2><p>Different strains, doses, and delivery routes matter. We explain what the studies measured, what they didn’t, and why a familiar brand name isn’t enough.</p><div class="actions">{btn('/gummies-vs-capsules/','Gummies vs. capsules')}{link('/probiotics/','Explore the research','text-link')}</div></div><div>{photo('uro-gummy','URO oral gummy bottle','Product example for label comparison. Manufacturer photo. Not an odor-treatment endorsement.')}</div></div></section><div class="wrap"><section class="section"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">A few common questions</span><h2>Less guesswork. More understanding.</h2></div></div><div class="faq"><details><summary>Do I need a special menopause wash?</summary><p>No special product is necessary for routine external care. Water is enough for many people; a plain fragrance-free cleanser may be an option if tolerated. Read our <a href="/gentle-care/">gentle-care guide</a>.</p></details><details><summary>Does “pH balanced” mean it treats vaginal odor?</summary><p>No. Product pH and internal vaginal conditions are different questions. A wash label cannot establish the cause of a symptom. <a href="/ingredient-labels/">See how to read the claims</a>.</p></details><details><summary>Can probiotic gummies replace a clinician’s visit?</summary><p>No. The research reviewed here does not establish retail gummies as a reliable treatment for menopause-related odor. <a href="/probiotics/">Our evidence guide</a> explains the limitations.</p></details></div></section></div><section class="section dark-band"><div class="wrap section-heading" style="margin-bottom:0"><div><span class="eyebrow">When something feels different</span><h2>You can bring it up.<br>We’ll help you prepare.</h2></div><div><p>Take a simple checklist to your appointment, with the changes you’ve noticed and the questions you want answered.</p><div class="actions">{btn('/appointment-checklist/','Open the appointment checklist')}</div></div></div></section>'''
layout('/','Menopause odor, gentle washes & probiotic evidence','Clear, source-linked information about menopause scent changes, external cleansing, and probiotic claims.',home)

wash_intro=f'''<div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / External washes</div><header class="article-head"><span class="eyebrow">Shop with the label in mind</span><h1>Compare body washes.</h1><p class="dek">Three fragrance-free options, with intended use and limitations made clear. This is a label comparison, not a clinical ranking.</p><p class="byline">Labels checked {DATE} · No hands-on or clinical testing</p></header>{callout('First, the option that costs nothing.','Water is sufficient for many people. If you choose a cleanser, use a plain fragrance-free option you tolerate on external skin, rinse well, and stop if it irritates. Never wash inside the vagina. <a href="/gentle-care/">Read the care guide</a>.')}{disclosure()}<div class="filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter wash comparison"><button class="filter" data-filter="all" aria-pressed="true">All options</button><button class="filter" data-filter="body" aria-pressed="false">General body washes</button><button class="filter" data-filter="intimate" aria-pressed="false">Labeled for the vulva</button></div><p id="filter-status" class="filter-status" aria-live="polite">3 products shown</p><div class="product-grid">{''.join(product(p) for p in shortlist)}</div><section class="section"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">Read beyond the name</span><h2>Why some familiar products<br>aren’t on this shortlist.</h2></div><p>These are ingredient observations, not claims that everyone will react to a product.</p></div><div class="link-grid"><div class="link-card"><h3>Dove Sensitive ≠ Ultra Sensitive</h3><p>The ordinary Sensitive Body Wash page lists fragrance. Our comparison uses the separate fragrance-free Ultra Sensitive variant.</p>{link('/ingredient-labels/','See the label checks')}</div><div class="link-card"><h3>Menopause branding needs a closer look.</h3><p>Vagisil’s Menopause Support Mousse page lists fragrance despite conflicting fragrance-free copy. We excluded it from the fragrance-free shortlist.</p>{link('/ingredient-labels/','Read the ingredient notes')}</div></div>{callout('What we have not established','None of these product pages establishes that its cleanser treats menopause-related vaginal odor, infection, or GSM. We have not tested irritation, scent, or effectiveness ourselves.',True)}</section></div>'''
layout('/external-washes/','Compare gentle external washes','Compare fragrance-free body washes and external vulva cleansers, including ingredients, intended use and evidence limits.',wash_intro)

for p in shortlist:
    i=wash_info[p['id']]
    body=f'''{photo(i['image'],esc(p['name']), 'Manufacturer packaging. Product information, not a treatment endorsement.')}<span class="badge">{i['tag']}</span><h2>Before you choose it</h2><p>{i['watch']}</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Product</th><td>{esc(p['name'])}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Size checked</th><td>{i['size']}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Intended use</th><td>{esc(p['route'])}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Fragrance check</th><td>{esc(p['fragrance'])}</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Ingredient notes</h2><p>{esc(p.get('ingredients',p.get('ingredients_facts','')))}</p><p>Ingredients can change. Compare the exact product name and the current package, not just a retailer thumbnail. Fragrance-free does not mean irritation is impossible.</p><h2>What this product does not establish</h2><p>{esc(p['cautions_claim_gaps'])}</p><p>This is a label-based comparison. We have not tested the product.</p><p>If you are shopping for genital skin, read the <a href="/gentle-care/">gentle vulva-care guide</a> first. A body-wash label does not establish suitability for every body area.</p><h2>Check the current product</h2>{disclosure()}<p>Price, stock, shipping and purchase terms are shown by the retailer or manufacturer. We omit a fixed price here because it can change.</p><div class="actions">{btn(p['official_url'],'View at '+i['brand'])}{btn('/external-washes/','Compare all washes',True)}</div><h2>Product source</h2><p>{link(p['official_url'],'Official manufacturer page')} · Checked {DATE}.</p>{sources('S1','S2')}'''
    article('/washes/'+p['id']+'/',p['name'],i['text'],body,'Product label notes')

article('/odor-and-menopause/','A change in scent deserves clarity.','What menopause can change, what a wash cannot tell you, and how to choose a useful next step.',f'''{photo('towels','Folded towels in a bathroom')}{callout('A scent is not a hygiene score.','A mild natural scent is normal. A new, strong, or persistent change is worth discussing, especially with other symptoms.')}<h2>Start by separating the questions</h2><p>People often use “down there” to describe several areas at once. The vulva is the external genital skin. The vagina is internal. Sweat on skin, urine-related concerns, and a change in vaginal discharge are different things to describe to a clinician.</p><p>You do not have to work out the cause yourself. Notice where you seem to detect the change, when it happens, and whether anything else feels different.</p><h2>Where menopause fits</h2><p>Lower estrogen can affect vaginal tissues and their environment. Dryness, discomfort with sex, irritation, and some urinary symptoms can be part of genitourinary syndrome of menopause, often shortened to GSM. Similar symptoms can have other causes, so menopause is context, not an automatic diagnosis.</p><h2>What a wash can and cannot do</h2><p>A tolerated external cleanser can remove surface residue during washing. It cannot establish why vaginal scent has changed, diagnose an infection, or replace treatment for a medical condition. More frequent washing or fragrance can add irritation without answering the underlying question.</p><h2>Three useful next steps</h2><div class="link-grid"><div class="link-card"><h3>Keep care simple</h3><p>Water, gentle external cleansing, and avoiding scented products are a practical starting point.</p><a href="/gentle-care/">Gentle vulva care</a></div><div class="link-card"><h3>Describe the change</h3><p>Bring timing, associated symptoms, and products you use to an appointment.</p><a href="/appointment-checklist/">Open the checklist</a></div></div>{callout('Get symptoms checked','New or persistent odor, altered discharge, burning, itching, or pain should be assessed. Any bleeding after menopause needs evaluation. <a href="/when-to-get-checked/">Read the care signposts</a>.',True)}{sources('S1','S2','S3','S12')}''')

article('/gentle-care/','A gentler external-care routine.','Simple care does not need to become a long shopping list.',f'''{photo('routine','Towels and a dispenser in warm light')}<h2>Outside and inside are different</h2><p>Routine cleaning is for the outside. Avoid douching or putting cleanser inside the vagina. Water is sufficient for many people; if you prefer cleanser, choose a plain fragrance-free option that you tolerate and use it externally.</p><h2>Keep the routine comfortable</h2><ul><li>Use water and gentle handling. Avoid vigorous rubbing or scrubbing.</li><li>If using a cleanser, rinse it away thoroughly and stop if it stings, dries, or irritates.</li><li>Dry gently. Change out of damp clothing when practical.</li><li>Avoid fragranced intimate sprays, deodorants, and internal cleansing products.</li></ul><p>There is no universal product that every person needs. If you have a vulvar skin condition or ongoing discomfort, ask for a routine suited to that condition.</p><h2>Water-only care</h2><p>That concern came up in our community research. Feeling clean and having no detectable scent are not the same goal. A stronger cleanser is not a diagnostic tool. If odor persists or has changed, a clinician can help identify what is happening.</p><h2>If you still want a cleanser</h2><p>Look at the exact formula, intended body area, fragrance ingredients, and how your skin responds. “Intimate,” “natural,” or “menopause” on a bottle does not establish treatment benefit.</p><div class="actions">{btn('/external-washes/','Compare the labels')}{link('/ingredient-labels/','Understand product claims','text-link')}</div>{sources('S1','S2','S12')}''')

article('/ingredient-labels/','The label matters more than the promise.','How to compare fragrance, intended use, and pH claims without getting lost in marketing.',f'''<h2>Start with intended use</h2><p>Check whether a product is a body cleanser, an external vulva cleanser, an oral supplement, or a vaginal product. These routes are not interchangeable. Our wash comparison covers external products only.</p><h2>“Sensitive” does not mean fragrance-free</h2><p>We checked the exact current variants, not just the brand. Dove’s Ultra Sensitive Body Wash says fragrance-free and lists 10 ingredients. Its ordinary Sensitive Body Wash lists fragrance. Similar names can hide a meaningful formula difference.</p><h2>“Natural” can still include a scent</h2><p>Good Clean Love Balance lists Natural Lavender Flavor and describes a floral aroma. That is different from fragrance-free. Botanical ingredients can still matter to someone avoiding scented products.</p><h2>When a page contradicts itself</h2><p>At our November 12, 2026 check, the Vagisil Menopause Support Probiotic Mousse Wash page included fragrance-free wording, “light botanical scent,” and Fragrance in the ingredient list. We treat the fragrance status as conflicting and exclude it from the fragrance-free shortlist pending clarification.</p>{callout('What this means for shopping','Use the current package and the complete ingredient list. If the label and page disagree, ask the manufacturer before buying. We do not resolve a contradiction by choosing the most favorable claim.',True)}<h2>Product pH is not a diagnosis</h2><p>A bottle’s “pH balanced” wording does not show that it changes internal vaginal pH or treats symptoms. Vaginal pH may rise after menopause. A home pH reading cannot identify the cause of an odor or reliably rule out infection.</p><h2>“Probiotic” on a wash is a separate claim</h2><p>A listed ferment does not, by itself, establish living probiotic organisms, viable count, or clinical benefit. Evidence for an oral or vaginal formulation cannot simply be applied to a rinse-off cleanser.</p><h2>Product pages we checked</h2><ul class="source-list">{''.join('<li>'+link(p['official_url'],esc(p['name']))+'</li>' for p in P['cleansers'] if p['id'] in ['dove-ultra-sensitive','dove-sensitive','good-clean-love-balance','vagisil-menopause-mousse'])}</ul>{sources('S5','S6','S12')}''')

oral_ids=['rephresh-pro-b','uro-gummies','love-good-girl']
oral_images={'rephresh-pro-b':'rephresh','uro-gummies':'uro-gummy','love-good-girl':'love-probiotic'}
oral_cards=''
for p in P['oral_supplements']:
    if p['id'] not in oral_ids:continue
    oral_cards+=f'''<article class="product"><div class="product-art"><img src="/assets/{oral_images[p['id']]}.webp" alt="{esc(p['name'],quote=True)} manufacturer packaging" loading="lazy"></div><div class="product-body"><span class="badge caution">Label example · not a treatment pick</span><h3>{esc(p['name'])}</h3>{rating(p['id'])}<p>{esc(p['label_facts'])}</p><p><strong>Evidence limit:</strong> No menopause-odor benefit for this finished retail product was established in this review.</p><div class="product-links">{link(p['official_url'],'Read the manufacturer label')}</div></div></article>'''

article('/probiotics/','Probiotics: what does the evidence actually say?','Promising mechanisms, small studies, and retail product claims are different levels of evidence.',f'''{callout('The practical answer','Our targeted review did not establish retail probiotic gummies as a reliable treatment for menopause-related odor. A supplement is optional and does not replace assessment of symptoms.')}<h2>Ask what was measured</h2><p>A change in bacteria or a microscopic score is not automatically a change in smell, discomfort, or quality of life. A useful study needs to match the product, the people using it, and the result being promised.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Study</th><th scope="col">What it tested</th><th scope="col">What it cannot establish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Oral trial, 2008</td><td>72 postmenopausal participants; GR-1/RC-14 capsules for 14 days. A microscopic Nugent score improved.</td><td>Odor relief, long-term benefit, or gummy effectiveness. We accessed the abstract, not the full paper.</td></tr><tr><td>Vaginal trial, 2014</td><td>14 postmenopausal participants; short vaginal administration. Microbial changes occurred without significant Nugent-score or pH benefit.</td><td>Benefit from taking oral capsules or gummies. This was a very small study.</td></tr><tr><td>Lactin-V trial, 2020</td><td>A specific vaginal formulation after antibiotics in 228 premenopausal women with BV; fewer recurrences.</td><td>A general menopause-odor claim for an over-the-counter oral supplement.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Compare five things before comparing brands</h2><p>Check the exact strain identifiers, total and per-strain dose when disclosed, delivery route, study population, and measured outcome. Higher CFU is not automatically better. Species names alone do not identify a studied strain.</p><p>The 2021 CDC BV treatment guideline does not support available probiotic products as a substitute for or addition to BV treatment, while discussing a specific promising vaginal formulation separately. That distinction matters.</p><h2>Gummies need their own evidence</h2><p>A gummy can use different organisms and added vitamins from a capsule sold by the same brand. A shared brand name does not make the products clinically interchangeable.</p><div class="actions">{btn('/gummies-vs-capsules/','Compare gummies and capsules')}</div><h2>Label examples, not an efficacy ranking</h2><p>These examples show what can be checked on a label. They are not recommendations to buy a supplement for odor.</p>{disclosure()}<div class="product-grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr">{oral_cards}</div><h2>Questions before spending money</h2><ul><li>Is there a study of this finished formula in people like me, measuring the symptom I want to improve?</li><li>What other vitamins or supplements would I be taking alongside it?</li><li>Could symptoms need diagnosis instead of a supplement trial?</li><li>What is the full recurring cost, and is checkout a subscription?</li></ul><p>People who are seriously ill or immunocompromised should be especially cautious about live microbial supplements and discuss them with their clinician.</p>{sources('S4','S6','S7','S8','S10','S11')}''','The evidence library')

article('/gummies-vs-capsules/','Gummies vs. capsules: check the actual formula.','Convenience is a preference. Evidence depends on more than the format.',f'''{photo('uro-gummy','URO pink pineapple gummy bottle','A manufacturer product image used to explain formula differences.')}<h2>A real example: URO</h2><p>At the time checked, URO capsules and URO gummies had different organisms, amounts, and added ingredients. They are not simply two ways to take the same formula.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Label detail</th><th>URO capsules</th><th>URO gummies</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Organisms listed</td><td>Four Lactobacillus species; no strain IDs in the retrieved panel</td><td>Bacillus coagulans SNZ 1969</td></tr><tr><td>Total CFU per labeled serving</td><td>5 billion</td><td>1 billion</td></tr><tr><td>Other highlighted ingredients</td><td>XOS prebiotic</td><td>Vitamins C and D3; pineapple and sea-buckthorn powders</td></tr><tr><td>Format and route</td><td>Oral capsules</td><td>Oral gummies</td></tr><tr><td>Menopause-odor benefit established here?</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These are label facts, not a dosing recommendation or proof that one formula is better. Formulas may change.</p><h2>What to compare on any supplement</h2><ul><li><strong>Strain:</strong> look beyond “Lactobacillus” to an identifier such as GR-1.</li><li><strong>Viable amount:</strong> check CFU, storage instructions, and whether the stated amount is maintained through expiration.</li><li><strong>Added ingredients:</strong> gummies may add sugar, flavoring, or vitamins; capsules can have other excipients.</li><li><strong>Evidence match:</strong> confirm the route, formula, population, and endpoint in the cited study.</li><li><strong>Purchase terms:</strong> compare one-time cost with the renewal price, serving count, and cancellation terms.</li></ul>{callout('Do not buy a format as a promise','Neither “gummy” nor “capsule” establishes relief from vaginal odor. Persistent symptoms need assessment, regardless of what a product advertises.',True)}<h2>Labels behind the comparison</h2><ul class="source-list"><li>{link('https://opositiv.com/products/uro-vaginal-probiotic-capsules/','URO capsules: manufacturer page')}</li><li>{link('https://opositiv.com/products/vaginal-probiotic-gummy','URO gummies: manufacturer page')}</li></ul>{sources('S6','S7')}''','The evidence library')

article('/when-to-get-checked/','When to get a change checked.','A shopping guide should also help you recognize when shopping is not the next step.',f'''<h2>Arrange a clinician’s assessment</h2><p>Bring up new or persistent odor, unusual discharge, itching, burning, pain when urinating, discomfort with sex, or symptoms that keep returning. You do not need to be certain of the cause before making an appointment.</p>{callout('Bleeding after menopause needs evaluation','Any vaginal bleeding after menopause should be assessed, even if it happens once or is only a small amount.',True)}<h2>When to seek urgent help</h2><p>Pelvic pain with fever or unusual discharge needs urgent assessment. Severe or worsening pelvic pain, fainting, or heavy bleeding warrants emergency help. This is a general signpost, not a complete triage checklist.</p><h2>What you can bring</h2><p>Write down when the change began, other symptoms, whether it comes and goes, and the products or treatments you use. A picture of a product’s ingredient label can be more useful than remembering the brand name alone.</p><div class="actions">{btn('/appointment-checklist/','Open the appointment checklist')}</div><h2>While you are waiting</h2><p>Keep external care gentle. Avoid trying to cover the smell with fragrance or using a pH strip as a diagnosis. If symptoms worsen, seek advice sooner rather than waiting for a product to work.</p>{sources('S1','S2','S3','S5','SUP1')}''')

checkitems=['When did the change start? Is it constant, intermittent, or linked to a particular activity?','Where do I notice it: external skin, underwear, urine leakage, or vaginal discharge? It is okay to be unsure.','Have I noticed discharge changes, itching, burning, dryness, pain, or bleeding?','What washes, wipes, pads, laundry products, medications, hormones, and supplements am I using?','What have I already tried, and did it help or cause discomfort?','What could explain the symptoms, and what examination or tests might help?','Could menopause-related tissue changes be involved, and what care options fit my history?','When should I follow up, and what changes should prompt earlier help?']
article('/appointment-checklist/','Make the appointment easier to start.','A short checklist for a conversation that deserves straightforward answers.',f'''<p>You can say: “I’ve noticed a change in scent, and I’d like help figuring out what is causing it.” You do not need to apologize or diagnose it yourself.</p><ul class="checklist">{''.join(f'<li><label><input type="checkbox"><span>{esc(t)}</span></label></li>' for t in checkitems)}</ul><p class="note-small">Checkboxes stay only in this open page and are not submitted or saved. Use a private paper note for personal health details.</p><div class="actions"><button class="btn" data-print>Print this checklist</button>{link('/when-to-get-checked/','When to seek help sooner','text-link')}</div>{callout('A conversation aid, not a diagnostic tool','Use this to organize your questions. A clinician can interpret your symptoms, medical history, examination, and any needed testing.')}<h2>Where the prompts came from</h2><p>These original editorial prompts reflect the practical questions seen in community discussions and the symptom categories in the care sources below. They are not a validated clinical questionnaire.</p>{sources('S1','S2','S3')}''')

article('/our-method/','A clearer way to research intimate care.','Visible sources, specific product labels, and room to say “we don’t know.”',f'''<h2>What this site is</h2><p>Menopause Wash is a private editorial concept for an independent information and product-comparison site. It is not a medical practice. The current draft was developed with AI-assisted research and has not received a clinician’s review.</p><h2>Three kinds of information, kept separate</h2><ul><li><strong>Clinical sources:</strong> professional organizations, public-health guidance, and original studies inform care explanations.</li><li><strong>Product sources:</strong> manufacturer pages and packaging establish label facts. They do not independently prove marketing claims.</li><li><strong>Community research:</strong> Reddit discussions and accessible YouTube material help us find useful questions. They cannot establish how common a symptom is or whether a treatment works.</li></ul><h2>What we actually accessed</h2><p>Our November 12 research included 15 Reddit discussions, reading original posts and selected public comments. Twelve concerned menopause or perimenopause; three concerned adjacent supplement topics. Seven YouTube-linked items were reviewed through indexed descriptions, transcript material, and related creator content. No video footage, audio, or YouTube comments were reviewed.</p><p>X searches did not yield a reliable collection of relevant posts. We do not claim to have established X trends. This was a targeted qualitative review, not a representative survey or systematic review.</p><h2>How products enter the comparison</h2><p>We check the exact variant, ingredient list, intended route, and conflicting page copy. The initial wash shortlist favors verified fragrance-free labeling. No product earns a place because its affiliate commission is higher.</p><p>We have not performed hands-on, irritation, laboratory, or clinical testing. Our source-linked Reddit sentiment scores summarize selected discussions using a disclosed calculation. They are not customer star ratings, clinical scores or product tests. <a href="/reddit-recommendations/#ratings">See the scoring method and every thread assessment.</a> We do not invent clinician approvals or customer experiences. An absence of evidence in this review is not proof that no study exists anywhere.</p><h2 id="photography">Photography</h2><p>The circular portraits beside Reddit quotations are AI-created illustrations. They do not depict the commenters or anyone endorsing a product. Each quotation is attributed to its actual Reddit username and linked to its source, with the photo disclosure beside it.</p><p>Bathroom scenes are illustrative, not recommended products or evidence of results.</p><ul class="source-list"><li>{link('https://unsplash.com/photos/a-close-up-of-a-bottle-of-soap-and-a-towel-on-a-table-DvAbratkrrY','Yukon Haughton / Unsplash')}: warm bathroom scene.</li><li>{link('https://unsplash.com/photos/a-stack-of-towels-sitting-next-to-a-soap-dispenser-013434jqmho','Lucifer Abraham / Unsplash')}: folded towels.</li><li>{link('https://unsplash.com/license','Unsplash license')}: stock-photography terms.</li></ul><p>Product photos come from the manufacturers linked beside the products. They are included in this private concept for reference; public commercial reuse rights have not been confirmed.</p><h2>How this could be funded</h2><p>The intended model is retailer and brand affiliate commissions with prominent disclosure. The current concept has no active affiliate tracking or verified program enrollment.</p><div class="actions">{btn('/sources/','Explore the source library')}{link('/affiliate-disclosure/','Read the disclosure','text-link')}</div>''','About this project')

sourcehtml=''.join(f'<div class="source-tile"><h3>{link(s["url"],esc(s["title"]))}</h3><p>{esc(s.get("organization",s.get("authors","Research")))}</p><small>Access: {esc('Abstract only' if 'abstract' in s['access_depth'].lower() else ('Indexed official-page text; full page not accessed' if 'indexed official-page' in s['access_depth'].lower() else ('Full guideline text' if 'guideline PDF' in s['access_depth'] else 'Full source text')))}</small></div>' for s in S.values())
article('/sources/','Follow the sources.','Primary sources and authoritative guidance behind the care and evidence pages.',f'''<p>These links support the explanations on this site. They are not endorsements of the products we discuss. Access limitations are included so you can distinguish full text from an abstract or indexed extract.</p>{sourcehtml}<h2>Product and community sources</h2><p>Each product page links to its manufacturer. The developer research package contains the full dated product, affiliate, and social-research ledgers, including contradictory labels and access limits.</p><p>Clinical sources and manufacturer formulas can change. This concept reflects a targeted check on {DATE}.</p>''','Research library')

article('/affiliate-disclosure/','How product links work.','A commercial model should be easy to understand.',f'''<h2>Current status</h2><p>This private concept uses ordinary manufacturer links. No Amazon Associates ID or other affiliate tracking ID has been connected, and no program enrollment has been verified. We do not earn a commission from the links configured in this version.</p><h2>The planned model</h2><p>After enrollment, selected product links may earn a commission when a reader buys. A plain-language disclosure will appear near recommendations and purchase links, not just on this page. Amazon’s required Associate statement will be added only after enrollment.</p><h2>Editorial independence</h2><p>Products should be compared using the same criteria whether or not a link earns money. A product’s commission does not establish its quality, safety, or effectiveness. The no-purchase option remains part of the care guidance.</p><h2>Retailer terms</h2><p>Prices, availability, subscriptions, shipping, and returns are set by the seller. Check the final checkout terms, especially whether a displayed offer renews automatically.</p><h2>Product review status</h2><p>Our current comparisons are based on published labels and research. We have not personally tested products and do not represent manufacturer claims as clinical findings.</p>''','Transparency')

article('/privacy/','Privacy in this concept.','No symptom submission, accounts, or email collection has been added.',f'''<h2>What the pages do</h2><p>The site uses static pages and small browser controls for navigation, product filters, and a printable checklist. The checklist is not submitted or stored by this site. Reloading the page clears it.</p><h2>What has not been added</h2><p>No advertising pixels, analytics scripts, email signup, health questionnaire, or custom account system have been implemented.</p><h2>Other services</h2><p>The hosting provider may process standard request and access information. Fonts are loaded from Google Fonts, which receives a request from your browser. Product and source links take you to other websites with their own privacy practices.</p><h2>Before a public launch</h2><p>The operator’s identity, contact channel, hosting practices, and any future analytics or affiliate integrations need to be reflected in a complete privacy notice. This page describes the current private concept, not a finalized public privacy policy.</p>''','Transparency')

